{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTLfr3jg1bmnQmguAUM1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`test_large_sequence` fails because its expected value looks wrong, not because `invoice_number` is broken.\n\n- `invoice_number(2026, 12345)` returns `\"INV-2026-12345\"`. The format `{seq:04}` pads to a minimum of 4 digits and never truncates, so a 5-digit sequence comes out in full (`invoice.py:5`).\n- The test expects `\"INV-2026-2345\"`. That drops the leading `1`, which is what you'd get by truncating to the last 4 digits.\n\nI ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest`. `test_format` passes and `test_large_sequence` fails with `'INV-2026-12345' != 'INV-2026-2345'`.\n\nWhich behavior you want decides the fix:\n1. **Numbers wider than 4 digits are allowed.** This is the current behavior. Change the test's expected value to `\"INV-2026-12345\"`.\n2. **The sequence must be exactly 4 digits and wrap around.** Change the code to `seq % 10000`. I don't recommend this, because sequence 12345 would then collide with 2345 and give two invoices the same number.\n\nI haven't changed anything. Tell me which behavior you want and I'll make the change."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":519,"cache_read_input_tokens":39475,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":519},"output_tokens":405,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6102d66-c739-46cd-91bf-02f58601cd84","uuid":"e450359d-011e-4112-a2a4-2a2e725fe5e5","timestamp":"2026-10-27T20:03:21.729Z","request_id":"req_011CgTLfqobqHMfuQnUnZ7Nh"}
